            "width": 686,
            "height": 646,
            "visibility": "show_all",
            "draggable": true,
            "presentation_state": "show_all",
            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "perm counts the ORDERED ways of picking some items from a larger set: how many different sequences you could make by picking operand items from input items.\n\n7 arriving at \"perm 2\" sends out 42: there are 7 choices for the first pick and 6 left for the second, and 7 x 6 = 42. Order counts, so gold-then-silver is a different result from silver-then-gold - 42 possible podiums for the top two of 7 runners. \"perm 3\" on 7 gives 210 (7 x 6 x 5).\n\nPicking every item (operand equal to the input) counts every possible arrangement:\n5 items can be put in order 120 ways.\n\nperm works on single whole numbers only. Fractions are cut down to whole numbers;\nan array or tensor does not raise an error, but the result is a meaningless 0 or 1.\n\nSYNTAX:\nperm <operand: int>\n\nEXAMPLES:\nperm 2    ordered pairs\nperm 3    first, second and third places\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nin:\nHow many items there are to choose from. \nReceiving data here triggers the calculation.\n\noperand:\nHow many of them to pick. \nSetting it does not send anything - it waits for the next value at \"in\".\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nresult:\nThe number of different ordered picks.\n\nIf you pick more than there are, or either number is negative, the result is 0. The count grows very fast: 13 items in order already exceed six billion, more than a number box can show.\n\nSEE ALSO:\ncombination   the same count when order does not matter",
                    "value_type": "string"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 678,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 630,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "string"
                }
            },
            "text": "perm counts the ORDERED ways of picking some items from a larger set: how many different sequences you could make by picking operand items from input items.\n\n7 arriving at \"perm 2\" sends out 42: there are 7 choices for the first pick and 6 left for the second, and 7 x 6 = 42. Order counts, so gold-then-silver is a different result from silver-then-gold - 42 possible podiums for the top two of 7 runners. \"perm 3\" on 7 gives 210 (7 x 6 x 5).\n\nPicking every item (operand equal to the input) counts every possible arrangement:\n5 items can be put in order 120 ways.\n\nperm works on single whole numbers only. Fractions are cut down to whole numbers;\nan array or tensor does not raise an error, but the result is a meaningless 0 or 1.\n\nSYNTAX:\nperm <operand: int>\n\nEXAMPLES:\nperm 2    ordered pairs\nperm 3    first, second and third places\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nin:\nHow many items there are to choose from. \nReceiving data here triggers the calculation.\n\noperand:\nHow many of them to pick. \nSetting it does not send anything - it waits for the next value at \"in\".\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nresult:\nThe number of different ordered picks.\n\nIf you pick more than there are, or either number is negative, the result is 0. The count grows very fast: 13 items in order already exceed six billion, more than a number box can show.\n\nSEE ALSO:\ncombination   the same count when order does not matter"
        },
